    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • The Volkswagen Group's Affordable BEV Quartet Has Almost Reached 150,000 Orders\nSon's Barn-Found Oldsmobile Needs Restoration After Spending 47 Years in Storage\n45 photos\nPhoto: eBay seller 383-runner\nHere’s something you don’t see every day. This 1966 Oldsmobile spent no more, no less than 47 years locked in a barn, away from the road and away from humanity. The reason why it’s online is pretty simple: it’s now ready to go, so if you’re somehow interested in restoring a nice Olds, this Starfire is certainly worth checking out.\nBought by the seller’s son in March this year, this Oldsmobile is obviously a project whose only option is a complete restoration.\nThe closer you get, and the deeper you dig, the more problems you’ll find, but these shouldn’t necessarily be a reason to walk away for those who’ve come across classic cars before. An engine that no longer starts and metal corrosion are something very common in this space.\nPhoto: eBay seller 383-runner The Starfire could only be had as a coupe in 1966. It was the only model available in a single configuration, as every other Oldsmobile was released in multiple body styles. For example, the Ninety-Eight lineup included a luxury sedan, a Holiday sedan, a Holiday coupe, a Town sedan, and a convertible, while the Cutlass was offered in Supreme, Holiday Coupe, convertible, Sports Coupe, and Celebrity Sedan versions.\nOn the other hand, the Starfire received the second most powerful engine in the lineup. It was fitted with a 425ci engine rated at 375 horsepower, which used a Quadrajet carburetor and premium fuel. It was standard on the Starfire and optional on all Ninety-Eight and Delta 88 models.\nThe top engine this year was the Toronado Rocket, which sported a similar displacement but with a maximum output of 385 horsepower. This was standard on the Toronado.\nThe rest of the engines developed less power than the Starfire unit, and Oldsmobile continued to offer a six specifically for buyers interested in efficiency. The Action-Line 6 sported a displacement of 250 cubic inches and developed 155 horsepower, running on regular fuel and being offered only to F-85 buyers.\nPhoto: eBay seller 383-runner Oldsmobile praised the Starfire - not on every occasion, as the company had other cars to brag about, such as the Toronado - but quite often. The Starfire was fitted with thin-shell bucket seats and a floor-mounted console, and Oldsmobile wanted potential buyers to know about all of these.\n“This year Starfire sports a dashing new look all its own. And style is only a starter where this performer is concerned,’ the carmaker said when taking the wraps off the new Starfire.\nSure, a Starfire isn’t the most desirable collectible, but a specimen that checks all the right boxes can’t be ignored. And this restoration candidate appears to do this, with the seller revealing that the vehicle still wears the original paint.\nPhoto: eBay seller 383-runner This is big, as the Starfire is likely unrestored, so whoever refreshes this car will be the first one to return it to its day-one glory. It goes without saying that this Oldsmobile deserves a restoration to factory specifications, and altering it wouldn’t make any sense, especially considering it still has the original engine under the hood.\nIt’s still fitted with a 425 and an automatic transmission, though you’re not getting quite the best news when opening the hood. The engine no longer starts, but if you want to see the glass half-full, you should know that it still turns over. There’s life left in this engine, and it’s up to your mechanic to give it a boost and bring the 425 back to a running shape.\nThe car also sports the original interior. “Basically, it’s untouched,” the seller explains.\nOn the other hand, we’re not getting any information about the condition of the metal. Nearly five decades in storage equals enough time for the rust invasion to do its thing.\nThe photos revealing the underside suggest typical rust, but my recommendation is that you raise it on a lift to figure out what is happening there. The trunk exhibits only surface problems, so there’s a chance that the vehicle wasn’t forced to deal with high humidity levels in that barn where it’s been sitting for so long.\nPhoto: eBay seller 383-runner Eventually, the price is the one that makes a huge difference, and in the case of a Starfire, this happens for two reasons. First, this is a project that will burn more dollars once the restoration begins, and second, a Starfire isn’t exactly the most sought-after classic.\nThis means it must be priced correctly to find a new home. The seller believes at least $6,000 is a fair price for this Oldsmobile, but they obviously expect to get more dollars on the car as part of this no-reserve auction. With 3 days remaining until the auction ends, the vehicle is still at zero bids, so it might be quite a challenge for this Olds to leave for a new garage on this attempt.\nBut as with every no-reserve auction, potential buyers might be waiting until the last few minutes to submit their offers to avoid a fierce battle. Whoever gets their hands on this car will have to pick it up from Kansas City.\nVia // eBay\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nOldsmobile 1966 Oldsmobile Oldsmobile Starfire Starfire\nAbout the author: Bogdan Popa\nBogdan keeps an eye on how technology is taking over the car world. His long-term goals are buying an 18-wheeler because he needs more space for his kid’s toys, and convincing Google and Apple that Android Auto and CarPlay deserve at least as much attention as their phones. Full profile\nUP NEXT Elon Musk Wants Tesla To Double Its Production Capacity by 2030, Does This Even Make Sense?",
